      "text": "# How a Hash Map Works: A Detailed Explanation\n\nA hash map (also called hash table, dictionary, or associative array) is a data structure that stores key-value pairs and provides average-case O(1) lookup, insertion, and deletion. Below is a thorough walkthrough of its internals.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Components\n\nA hash map consists of:\n\n- **An array of buckets** (often called slots or bins). Each bucket can hold one or more entries.\n- **A hash function** that converts a key into an integer.\n- **A collision resolution strategy** for when two keys map to the same bucket.\n- **A load factor threshold** that triggers resizing.\n\nEach entry typically stores:\n- The key\n- The value\n- (Optionally) a cached hash code\n- (For chaining) a pointer to the next entry\n\n---\n\n## 2. Step-by-Step Operation\n\n### Step 1: Compute the Hash Code\n\nWhen you insert or look up a key `k`, the map first computes a hash code:\n\n```\nh = hash(k)\n```\n\nThe hash function should:\n- Be **deterministic** (same key \u2192 same hash).\n- Be **fast** to compute.\n- **Distribute keys uniformly",
